<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Pdfs on Nicole van der Hoeven</title><link>https://nicolevanderhoeven.com/tags/pdfs/</link><description>Recent content in Pdfs on Nicole van der Hoeven</description><generator>Hugo -- gohugo.io</generator><language>en-us</language><copyright>Nicole van der Hoeven</copyright><lastBuildDate>Fri, 14 Apr 2023 08:01:11 +0200</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://nicolevanderhoeven.com/tags/pdfs/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Organizing PDFs in Obsidian</title><link>https://nicolevanderhoeven.com/blog/20230414-organizing-pdfs-in-obsidian/</link><pubDate>Fri, 14 Apr 2023 08:01:11 +0200</pubDate><guid>https://nicolevanderhoeven.com/blog/20230414-organizing-pdfs-in-obsidian/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;There are many tools for organizing PDFs in Obsidian, but which one is the best? In this video, I go through several options, including DevonThink, Readwise, Skim, and Zotero. For my (non-academic) use case of organizing my TTRPG-related PDFs, I decided on Zotero&amp;ndash; but maybe something else will be better for you!&lt;/p&gt;
